  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 1019 PM MDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nAn upper level high over the Great Basin will weaken a bit on \nMon. Once again there is some mid level moisture embedded in weak \nNW flow aloft. As a result, can't rule out isold high based \nshowers over the high terrain. Meanwhile, with rising 850-700 mb \ntemps along with downslope low level flow, highs may tie or break \nrecords, as readings reach the mid 80's to around 90 over the \nplains.\n\nOn Tue, a weak disturbance will affect the area with some increase \nin mid level moisture.  Thus may see a slightly better chc of high \nbased showers/isold tstms over the higher terrain in the aftn. \nSome of this activity may affect portions of the I-25 Corridor by \nlate aftn or early evening. Over the rest of nern CO, it will \nremain dry with highs remaining 10 to 15 degrees above normal.\n\nBy Wed, the flow aloft will remain weak WNW. There will still be \nsome lingering mid level moisture over the higher terrain. Overall, \nwill keep in a slight chc of high based showers/isold tstms. Once \nagain highs will be 10 to 15 degrees above normal.\n\nFor Thu and Fri, the flow aloft will gradually become more zonal by \nFri.  Still expect mainly dry conditions both days.  The GFS tries \nto backdoor a cool front into nern CO on Fri while the ECMWF has \ndownslope low level flow as a sfc lee trough is over nern CO. \nOverall, still believe highs will remain 10 to 15 degrees above \nnormal across the plains both days.\n\nBy next weekend, the flow aloft will increase from the WSW as an \nupper level trough develops over the Pacific NW on Sat.  Should see \nanother day of warm and dry conditions over the area on Sat. \nMeanwhile, both the ECMWF and GFS keep the upper level trough to the \nNW of the area thru Sun. Both models show some subtropical \nmoisture embedded in the flow aloft which may lead to a slight chc\nof showers over portions of the higher terrain by aftn. Across \nthe plains, it looks dry with another day of above normal \ntemperatures.\n\nFor late Sun through Mon, there are varying solutions as to how fast \nor slow the upper level trough will be as it moves eastward. The GFS \nis faster while the ECMWF is slower by at least 24 hours. Naturally, \ndepending on the timing and how much it amplifies will have a \nsignificant impact on weather for next Mon and Tue.",
